        <mxCell id="legend-title" value="Cross-account resolution flow (Route 53 PHZ approach — recommended)" style="text;html=1;fontSize=12;fontStyle=1;" parent="1" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry x="215" y="678" width="600" height="20" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="legend-text" value="1. Network account creates the execute-api Interface VPCE (PrivateDnsEnabled=FALSE to avoid intercepting all regional API Gateway DNS).&#xa;2. Network account creates a Route 53 Private Hosted Zone scoped to the exact API ID (&lt;api-id&gt;.execute-api.&lt;region&gt;.amazonaws.com), with an A-alias record to the VPCE DNS name.&#xa;3. The PHZ is associated with the Application account&#39;s workload VPC (same-account association call, or authorize+associate+cleanup sequence if cross-account).&#xa;4. IDP&#39;s private API resource policy (in the Application account) is pinned to the central execute-api VPCE ID — must match exactly, or requests are denied.&#xa;5. On-prem/VPN browsers resolve the API hostname via a Route 53 Resolver Inbound Endpoint + corporate DNS conditional forwarder scoped to the API FQDN.&#xa;6. (Optional) Custom vanity domain: requires a SEPARATE step — sharing the API Gateway private custom domain&#39;s management policy via AWS RAM from the account&#xa;    that owns the domain to the account that owns the VPC endpoint, then granting execute-api:Invoke on the domain policy to that VPCE." style="text;html=1;fontSize=10;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" parent="1" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry x="215" y="700" width="1110" height="100" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
